## Design Patterns

### 1. Slowly Changing Dimensions (SCD)

**SCD Type 0 (No Change):**
```
Product_Key | Product_ID | Product_Name | Category
    1       |    100     |   Widget A   | Hardware
    
(Dimension never updated)
```

**SCD Type 1 (Overwrite):**
```sql
-- Update existing record
MERGE INTO dim_product t
USING staging_product s
ON t.product_id = s.product_id
WHEN MATCHED THEN
  UPDATE SET t.category = s.category;  -- Overwrites old value
```

**SCD Type 2 (Add New Record):**
```sql
-- Create new version with dates
Product_Key | Product_ID | Category (Old) | Category (New) | Start_Date | End_Date | Active
    1       |    100     |   Hardware     |    NULL       | 2024-01-01 | 2024-12-31 | 0
    2       |    100     |   NULL         |    Software   | 2025-01-01 | NULL       | 1

-- Insert logic
INSERT INTO dim_product (product_id, category, start_date, end_date, active)
SELECT @product_id, @new_category, GETDATE(), NULL, 1;

UPDATE dim_product
SET end_date = GETDATE(), active = 0
WHERE product_id = @product_id AND active = 1;
```

**SCD Type 3 (Add Column):**
```sql
-- Store both old and new values
ALTER TABLE dim_product ADD previous_category VARCHAR(100);

UPDATE dim_product
SET previous_category = category,
    category = new_category
WHERE product_id = @product_id;
```

### 2. Star Schema Loading

```
                    ┌─────────────┐
                    │ dim_date    │
                    └────────┬────┘
                             │
        ┌────────────────────┼────────────────────┐
        │                    │                    │
    ┌───▼──┐         ┌──────▼──────┐        ┌────▼────┐
    │      │         │             │        │         │
┌───┴──────┴──┐  ┌───┴─────────────┴───┐  ┌┴─────────┴────┐
│ dim_customer│  │   FACT_SALES      │  │ dim_product    │
└─────────────┘  │ (Central table)   │  └───────────────┘
                 │ ├─ date_key       │
                 │ ├─ customer_key   │
                 │ ├─ product_key    │
                 │ ├─ amount         │
                 │ ├─ quantity       │
                 │ └─ metrics        │
                 └───────────────────┘
```

**Loading Order:**
```
1. Load dimensions first (dim_date, dim_customer, dim_product)
2. Load fact table (depends on dimension keys)
3. Create/refresh aggregations

Why? Referential integrity constraints require dimension keys to exist first
```

### 3. Dimensional Modeling

**Grain of Fact Table:**
```
Fact_Sales grain: One row per transaction
Fact_DailySales grain: One row per product per day per store
Fact_MonthlySales grain: One row per product per month per store

More granular = More flexibility, More storage
Less granular = Better performance, Aggregated metrics only
```

