## Best Practices

### 1. Error Handling & Recovery

**Checkpoint Strategy:**
```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Extract 1000 records                    │
├─────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ CHECKPOINT: Record position = 1000      │
├─────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Transform 1000 records                  │
├─────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ CHECKPOINT: Staging loaded              │
├─────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Validate 1000 records                   │
├─────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ CHECKPOINT: Validation passed           │
├─────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Load 1000 records                       │
├─────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ CHECKPOINT: Data warehouse updated      │
└─────────────────────────────────────────┘

If failure at checkpoint 3:
→ Restart from checkpoint 2 (no re-extract needed)
```

**Idempotency:**
```sql
-- Always design loads to be idempotent
-- Safe to re-run without side effects

-- BAD: Non-idempotent
INSERT INTO sales_total (amount)
SELECT SUM(amount) FROM transactions;
-- Running twice doubles the amount!

-- GOOD: Idempotent
TRUNCATE TABLE sales_total;
INSERT INTO sales_total (amount)
SELECT SUM(amount) FROM transactions;

-- Or with MERGE:
MERGE INTO sales_total t
USING (SELECT SUM(amount) as amount FROM transactions) s
ON 1=1
WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET t.amount = s.amount
WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT VALUES (s.amount);
```

### 2. Monitoring & Alerting

**Key Metrics to Track:**
```
├─ Extraction Metrics
│  ├─ Records extracted
│  ├─ Extract duration
│  ├─ Extraction errors
│  └─ Source system availability
│
├─ Transformation Metrics
│  ├─ Records transformed
│  ├─ Transformation duration
│  ├─ Validation failures
│  ├─ Data quality issues
│  └─ Duplicate records detected
│
├─ Load Metrics
│  ├─ Records loaded
│  ├─ Load duration
│  ├─ Constraint violations
│  ├─ Load errors
│  └─ Data warehouse size
│
└─ Overall Metrics
   ├─ Total pipeline duration
   ├─ Success/failure rate
   ├─ SLA adherence
   └─ Cost per execution
```

**Alerting Rules:**
```
1. If load_duration > 1 hour → Alert
2. If validation_failure_count > 100 → Alert
3. If source_availability < 95% → Alert
4. If duplicate_count > threshold → Alert
5. If extract_record_count differs > 10% from expected → Alert
```

### 3. Data Quality Checks

**Pre-Load Validation:**
```sql
SELECT 
    CASE WHEN COUNT(*) = 0 THEN 'FAIL: No data' ELSE 'PASS' END as null_check,
    CASE WHEN COUNT(DISTINCT order_id) = COUNT(*) THEN 'PASS' ELSE 'FAIL: Duplicates' END as duplicate_check,
    CASE WHEN MIN(amount) >= 0 THEN 'PASS' ELSE 'FAIL: Negative amounts' END as range_check,
    CASE WHEN COUNT(DISTINCT order_date) > 0 THEN 'PASS' ELSE 'FAIL: All nulls' END as data_check
FROM staging_orders;
```

**Post-Load Validation:**
```sql
-- Row count comparison
SELECT 
    (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM staging_orders) as source_count,
    (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM fact_sales WHERE load_date = CURRENT_DATE) as target_count,
    CASE WHEN source_count = target_count THEN 'PASS' ELSE 'FAIL' END as reconciliation;
```

